course: dictionary
lesson: 9741
title: Sunshine: light, warmth and a friendly word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Sunshine usually means the light and warmth that come from the sun. «Suddenly he throws down his brush and digs his way up into the sunshine of a big meadow.» Here he is out in the warm light of the sun, so sunshine means the sun's light.
explain: Sunshine can also mean happiness or cheerfulness. Someone might call a cheerful friend «sunshine», and that is a friendly name, mostly used in Britain. The words around it tell you which meaning is right: is the sentence about the weather, or about a person?
explain: Look for clues. «Wind, sunshine and flowing water keep coming back, so they are called renewable.» The sentence is about energy and nature, so sunshine is the light and warmth from the sun. Always ask: does my meaning fit into the sentence?
fact: Sunshine is the light and warmth that come from the sun.
fact: Plants use sunshine to make food, a process called photosynthesis.
fact: Your body can make vitamin D when your skin is in sunshine.
fact: Sunshine carries ultraviolet rays, which can burn your skin, so in strong sunshine wear a shirt, sunscreen and a hat, find shade and wear sunglasses.
fact: Sunshine can also mean happiness or cheerfulness, and «sunshine» is a friendly name for someone, mostly British.
exercise: «Suddenly he throws down his brush and digs his way up into the sunshine of a big meadow.» What does sunshine mean here? | Rain falling on the grass | *The light and warmth from the sun | A dark, cold cave | A brush for painting
exercise: «Wind, sunshine and flowing water keep coming back, so they are called renewable.» What does sunshine mean here? | *Light and warmth from the sun | Happiness and cheerfulness | A friendly name for a person | Something that can be used up once
exercise: Dad says, «Cheer up, sunshine, your team is doing well!» What does sunshine mean in this sentence? | *A cheerful, happy person | The light from the sun | A pair of sunglasses | A cloud that hides the sun
test: Walk out of a dark cinema into «bright sunshine» and you squint. Why does your pupil shrink? | The sunshine makes your pupil grow bigger | *Less light gets in, because the iris is a muscle that controls how much light enters | Your eyes are tired from the film, so they close | You are about to sneeze
test: Slip, slop, slap, seek, slide is a safety rule for sunshine. What should you do in strong sunshine? | *Wear a hat and sunscreen, and find shade | Stay indoors with the curtains open | Wear a thick winter coat | Lie in the sun all day to get a tan
exam: Which statement about sunshine is true? | Sunshine is made of rain | *Plants use sunshine to make food | Sunshine only comes out at night | Sunshine can never burn your skin
